WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.551
Ask for every part of a picture at once, and each part

00:00:03.551 --> 00:00:04.843
can follow a different picture.

00:00:05.223 --> 00:00:08.491
Jeveration makes those choices agree, in one forward pass.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:11.657
Hi, I'm Ochre.

00:00:11.657 --> 00:00:15.411
I read the Jeveration paper, its code and its checkpoint, and ran it.

00:00:17.242 --> 00:00:21.229
A frozen Qwen vision language model reads the prompt, then one decision

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:22.558
token per image code.

00:00:22.938 --> 00:00:26.113
A head picks every code at once, and a decoder paints them.

00:00:27.992 --> 00:00:31.852
Sample every code on its own and each follows its own picture.

00:00:31.852 --> 00:00:34.747
The paper proves the miss equals the total correlation.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.554
So every position reads one shared random state, and they agree on one picture.

00:00:39.974 --> 00:00:42.918
A new state gives a different picture, still in one piece.

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:48.770
Trained on images directly, the model ignores the state.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.148
So a masked model learns the image distribution first.

00:00:52.548 --> 00:00:55.895
Sampled in one pass, it scores point three two on GenEval.

00:00:56.295 --> 00:01:00.625
Then the one-pass student is trained against that teacher, until its codes fit together.

00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:03.257
The released checkpoint reaches point seven four.

00:01:04.592 --> 00:01:07.709
The headline speedup is against a slow autoregressive model.

00:01:07.709 --> 00:01:11.864
Against the fastest unified model in the same table, it is about

00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:13.250
one point two times.

00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:18.408
On a three ninety it takes about a hundred and forty-five milliseconds,

00:01:18.408 --> 00:01:20.316
two thirds of it the backbone.

00:01:20.316 --> 00:01:22.860
And each position's choice is far from confident.

00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:28.183
The paper's own plots leave out Show-o Turbo, the closest unified model.

00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:32.131
On preference scores it sits below most one-step diffusion models.

00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:37.410
The same weights also segment rooms and estimate depth and normals.

00:01:38.692 --> 00:01:41.967
Our old ceiling of eight bits a call was Jev's interface.

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:43.754
One call here returns far more.

00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:46.120
Independent questions cannot carry structure.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.844
Coupled ones, on a shared state, can.

00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:52.383
It still writes no text, and options in one question still

00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:53.518
never read each other.

00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:58.671
Parallel decisions can agree, if they share a random state and learn

00:01:58.671 --> 00:02:01.023
from a teacher that knows the whole picture.

00:02:03.692 --> 00:02:08.007
One pass, one shared state, and a speedup worth reading against the right row.

00:02:08.007 --> 00:02:08.932
Thanks for watching!

00:02:09.352 --> 00:02:11.418
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:11.418 --> 00:02:12.008
I'm Ochre.

00:02:12.008 --> 00:02:12.303
Bye!
